{#
 # The destructive action, and whatever sits beside it.
 #
 # The body is for the neighbours — an "edit" link, most often — which have to live inside the form
 # so the group reads as one control strip. The delete button itself is always rendered: it is what
 # this component is for, and a call site that could omit it would be a form with nothing to submit.
 #
 # Confirmation goes through the shared modal, with no round-trip: the submit-confirm controller
 # injects the template below into the `modal` frame, and « Confirmer » points back at this form
 # through its `form` attribute — a submitter from outside the form is the proof of confirmation.
 # Without JS (and for BrowserKit) the form submits directly, as it always has.
 #
 # Below `sm` only the icon is left: a page title and this strip do not fit across 358 px, and the
 # header was wrapping onto a second line on every show page. `aria-label` carries the name there —
 # `ux:icon` renders an `aria-hidden` svg, so a bare icon would leave the button unnamed.
 #}
{% props action %}

{% set formId = 'delete' ~ action|replace({'/': '-'}) %}

<form
    id="{{ formId }}"
    method="post"
    action="{{ action }}"
    data-controller="submit-confirm"
    data-action="submit->submit-confirm#onSubmit"
>
    <input type="hidden" name="_token" data-controller="csrf-protection" value="{{ csrf_token('submit') }}">

    {#
     # `btn-group` and not `btn-group-sm` alone: the size class is `.btn-group-sm > .btn`, which does
     # not ask for the group and so was sizing the buttons from a container that laid nothing out —
     # the two sat side by side on a stray space of markup. With the group they are the one strip the
     # component is named for, and the size comes from one place instead of from the group for the
     # neighbours and from a `size` prop for the button below.
     #}
    <div class="btn-group btn-group-sm">
        {% block content %}{% endblock %}

        <twig:Button variant="danger" outline aria-label="Supprimer">
            <twig:ux:icon name="mdi:trash-can-outline" /> <span class="d-none d-sm-inline">Supprimer</span>
        </twig:Button>
    </div>

    {#
     # « Annuler » comes first and carries `autofocus`: `showModal()` focuses the first focusable
     # element otherwise, which would put the keyboard on the destructive button — Enter to submit,
     # Enter again to delete. The order in the source is the order of the tab ring, so the safe
     # choice is also the one reached first.
     #}
    <template data-submit-confirm-target="content">
        <div class="app-modal-body text-center">
            <twig:ux:icon name="mdi:alert-circle" class="text-warning" height="48" width="48" />

            <h2 class="fs-5 fw-semibold mt-2 mb-1" id="modal-title">Confirmation de la suppression</h2>
            <p>Êtes-vous sûr de vouloir supprimer cet élément&nbsp;?</p>

            {#
             # A column of two full-width buttons until sm, a centred row after. The two do not fit
             # side by side across the 342 px the dialog has on a phone, and left to wrap they came
             # out centred in two different widths, one under the other — which reads as two loose
             # controls rather than as a choice between two.
             #
             # The order stays the source order at every width, so the destructive button is the one
             # in reach of the thumb. Turning the column over would put the safe choice there, at the
             # price of a visual order that no longer matches the tab order — and the confirmation
             # this dialog *is* already stands between the member and the deletion.
             #}
            <div class="d-grid d-sm-flex flex-wrap justify-content-center gap-2">
                <form method="dialog" class="d-grid">
                    <twig:Button variant="secondary" outline autofocus>Annuler</twig:Button>
                </form>

                <twig:Button variant="danger" form="{{ formId }}">Confirmer la suppression</twig:Button>
            </div>
        </div>
    </template>
</form>
